\label{fig:episodes}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Noise Robustness on Noise Holter Benchmark}
Under severe N2 ambulatory noise (where QRS morphology is unrecognizable), uncalibrated models suffered severe false alarm degradation (ArNet2 false alarm rate: 62.5\%). The Quality-Aware SetOperator abstained on 77.8\% of N2 segments and 47.1\% of marginal N1 segments, driving the false alarm rate to \textbf{0.0\%} on evaluable windows (Figure~\ref{fig:noise}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/figure3_noise_abstention.png}
\caption{Noise robustness across Clean, N1, and N2 ambulatory noise tiers, demonstrating complete false alarm suppression via Chow's abstention.}
\label{fig:noise}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Control Specificity Audit (791.4 Hours Sinus Rhythm)}
Table~\ref{tab:controls} details the specificity audit across all 33 continuous 24-hour non-AF Holters (791.4 total monitoring hours). While baseline unsmoothed models suffered from false alarms in 42.4\% of patients, the Quality-Aware SetOperator ($M_5$) elevated patient specificity to \textbf{84.85\%} and suppressed mean false burden from 3.72\% down to \textbf{1.33\% points} with an average abstention rate of only 6.92\%.

