\subsection{Multi-Lead Invariance \& Channel Dropout}
Evaluating across 15 post-ablation Holters demonstrated that SetOperator Phase-KME is invariant to lead geometry. Burden MAE was 25.86\% for Dual-Lead Joint, 25.91\% for Lead 1 (CC5) alone, 25.79\% for Lead 2 (NASA) alone, and 25.82\% under 50\% random lead dropout. The maximal cross-configuration discrepancy was $<0.12\%$ points, proving that the continuous operator framework preserves performance even during ambulatory lead detachment.

\subsection{Wearable Monitoring Completeness \& Uncertainty Bounds}
Table~\ref{tab:completeness} reports the clinical impact of wearable wear degradation and selective abstention. Continuous 24h wear achieved 85.9\% completeness (due to 14.1\% noise abstention) and 100.0\% sensitivity for both $\ge 30$s and $\ge 6$min recurrences. For 16h daytime wear (58.0\% completeness), sensitivity remained 100.0\% on $\ge 30$s and 93.9\% on $\ge 6$min, with a mean ascertainment delay of 3.0 hours. However, when wear was restricted to intermittent spot checks (2 $\times$ 30s strips/day), sensitivity on actionable $\ge 6$-minute episodes collapsed to \textbf{0.0\%}, and \textbf{99.0\% of patients} had indeterminate recurrence status ($\Delta = 99.9\%$ pts).

